#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204658} :activity/id #uuid "9814d74f-0e92-514b-a1aa-376353566f3a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048960 :topic/id #uuid "cf9ab88a-f36a-55c9-9686-426ec7537bce" :topic/math-academy-id 4819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205231 :tutorial/content "Recall that a relation $R$ over a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nA \\times A = A^2\n$$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nMore generally, the two factors in the Cartesian product do not have to be the same set. This gives the following definition:\n\n> *A relation $R$ over $A$*and*$B$ is a subset of $A \\times B$.*\n> $R \\subseteq A\\times B$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{1, 2, 3, 4 \\}, \\qquad B = \\{4,5,6,7 \\}\n$$\n\nThe following set is a relation over $A$ and $B$.\n\n$$\nR = \\{(1,4), (1,5), (2, 4), (3,5) \\}\n$$\n\nFor each ordered pair $(x,y)\\in R$, we have that $x\\in A$ and $y\\in B$." :tutorial/id #uuid "ddc79848-4bf5-5db1-ae3e-a2982fa49f1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9045 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fe12c9b-a785-5ff2-acc0-a7ae46466e49" :step/math-academy-id 152710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205261 :tutorial/content "Given a relation $R \\subseteq A \\times B$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The **domain** of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R$.\n- The **range** of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R$.\n- The **codomain** of $R$ is any superset of the range.\n\nFor example, consider the sets\n\n$$\nA = \\{1, 2, 3, 4 \\}, B = \\{4,5,6,7 \\}\n$$\n\nand the relation $R\\subseteq A\\times B$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\{(1,4), (1,5), (2, 4), (3,5) \\}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the domain and range of this relation:\n\n- To find the domain, we consider the first components of the ordered pairs belonging to our relation:\n$R = \\big\\{(\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{1},4), (\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{1},5), (\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{2},4), (\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{3},5) \\big\\}$\nThe set of first components is $\\{1,1,2,3 \\} = \\{1,2,3 \\}$.\nHence, the *domain* of $R$ is $\\{1,2,3 \\}$.\n- To find the range, we consider the second components of the pairs belonging to our relation:\n$R = \\big\\{(1,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{4}), (1,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{5}), (2,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{4}), (3,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{5}) \\big\\}$\nThe set of second components is $\\{4,5,4,5 \\} = \\{4,5 \\}$.\nHence, the *range* of $R$ is $\\{4,5 \\}$.\n\nWe can visualize the domain and range using a mapping diagram, where each ordered pair is represented by the arrow starting at the first component and ending in the second component:\n\n![](images/ad/ad106faeb24496914f496d7e4b49131dee36223e18e380ab70e47f72e7a5bdad.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "33e2a0a7-6306-55cf-b03c-61ccbc2b75bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9206 :tutorial/title "The Domain and Range of a Relation"} :step/id #uuid "7117daf6-25fc-5196-b05d-2abced768660" :step/math-academy-id 152711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203697 :question/id #uuid "05bbf2f9-cf55-5d11-b0d5-b647147f5b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-14649"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41ac9782-cf60-5432-8bc9-51493a97c248" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050648}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204264}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Range of a Relation"} :step/id #uuid "6c32af3c-b74d-594c-a80d-a21db4c51e4d" :step/math-academy-id 152712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205237 :tutorial/content "If a relation $R$ is defined over\n\n$$\nA = \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_m \\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nB = \\{b_1, b_2, \\ldots b_n \\}\n$$\n\nof cardinalities $m$ and $n$, respectively, the **adjacency matrix** of $R$ is a $m \\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element $r_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{1, if [math]a_i \\: R \\: b_j[/math],; 0, otherwise\n$$\n\nFor example, let's construct the adjacent matrix for the relation\n\n$$\nS = \\{(1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (3,2) \\}\n$$\n\ndefined on\n\n$$\nA = \\{1,2,3 \\}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing down the elements of $A$ along the rows and columns of the matrix:\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  |  |\n| $2$ |  |  |  |\n| $3$ |  |  |  |\n\nTo fill in the matrix, we run over the ordered pairs in our relation:\n\n- Since $(1,2)$ belongs to the relation, we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and $2$nd column.\n- Since $(1,3)$ belongs to the relation, we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and $3$rd column.\n- Since $(2,1)$ belongs to the relation, we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and $1$st column.\n- Since $(3,2)$ belongs to the relation, we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and $2$nd column.\n\nAll the other cells should be filled with zeros. Therefore, we obtain the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "6c8df684-c8a7-51f6-a886-729af0b98a7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9073 :tutorial/title "Adjacency Matrix of a Relation"} :step/id #uuid "dd391215-207e-5716-9997-84a9eead9dcf" :step/math-academy-id 152715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204662 :step/content 